MathCAT (braille): the user's own signs over a braille code's table

MateX lets a user give their own braille sign for a symbol, over an
existing code: where Nemeth and UEB define no sign they leave it to the
transcriber, and a school or a teacher has its own conventions. MathCAT
reads its rules only from the archive compiled in (include-zip), so the
signs come through the API instead of a file:

- `set_braille_user_signs(text)` (interface.rs) takes unicode.yaml
  entries (` - "⊏": [t: "⠈⠸⠣"]`); "" clears them. A change invalidates the
  braille caches, as a new BrailleCode does.
- `read_files` reads them right after the code's short unicode table, so
  each replaces the code's sign for that character (the short table is
  looked up first). A mistake in them is logged and ignored: braille goes
  on with the code's own signs.

Applied by scripts/bootstrap.sh on top of upstream 85c89a37. Only for
callers that want it; not proposed upstream as it is.

